ice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.\n  34: \n  35:     Input structure\n  36:     The grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\n  37:     separate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n  38: \n  39:     Rule\n  40:     Build a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\n  41:     versions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\n  42:     rectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\n  43:     consecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\n  44:     bottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\n  45:     again separated by one background column.\n  46: \n  47:     Key insight\n  48:     The input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\n  49:     contributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\n  50:     preserving left-to-right object order.\n  51:     \"\"\"\n  52:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n  53:     bg = get_bg(g)\n  54:     fg_colors = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})\n  55:     assert len(fg_colors) == 1\n  56:     fg = fg_colors[0]\n  57: \n  58:     components = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, colors=[fg])\n  59:     assert components\n  60:     components = sorted(components, key=lambda obj: (obj[\"bbox\"][1], obj[\"bbox\"][0]))\n  61: \n  62:     dims: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n  63:     for obj in components:\n  64:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n  65:         h = r1 - r0 + 1\n  66:         w = c1 - c0 + 1\n  67:         assert obj[\"area\"] == h * w\n  68:         dims.append((h, w))\n  69: \n  70:     out_h = 10\n  71:     out_w = sum(max(h, w) for h, w in dims) + len(dims) - 1\n  72:     _assert_output_footprints_disjoint(dims)\n  73:     out = np.full((out_h, out_w), bg, dtype=int)\n  74: \n  75:     c = 0\n  76:     for h, w in dims:\n  77:         short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n  78:         assert long <= out_h\n  79:         out[:short, c : c + long] = fg\n  80:         c += long + 1\n  81: \n  82:     c = 0\n  83:     for h, w in dims:\n  84:         short, long = min(h, w), max(h, w)\n  85:         assert long <= out_h\n  86:         out[out_h - long : out_h, c : c + short] = fg\n  87:         c += short + 1\n  88: \n  89:     return out\nEND_FUNCTION\nFUNCTION: generate_input lines 226-231\n 226: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n 227:     for _ in range(MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS):\n 228:         result = _gen_attempt()\n 229:         if result is not None:\n 230:             return result\n 231:     raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {MAX_GENERATION_ATTEMPTS} attempts\")\nEND_FUNCTION\nEND_FILE\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py\nFUNCTION: main lines 34-130\n  34: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n  35:     \"\"\"\n  36:     Move colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n  37: \n  38:     Input structure:\n  39:     - bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n  40: \n  41:     Rule:\n  42:     - For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n  43:     - Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n  44: \n  45:     Key insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n  46:     \"\"\"\n  47:     g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n  48:     H, W = g.shape\n  49:     bg = int(get_bg(g))\n  50: \n  51:     # --- frame interior: largest bg component not touching boundary ---\n  52:     inner_bbox = largest_enclosed_component_bbox(g == int(bg), connectivity=4)\n  53:     assert not (inner_bbox is None)\n  54:     ir0, ic0, ir1, ic1 = map(int, inner_bbox)\n  55:     fr0, fc0, fr1, fc1 = int(ir0 - 1), int(ic0 - 1), int(ir1 + 1), int(ic1 + 1)\n  56: \n  57:     # --- divider columns (fixed) on the left side ---\n  58:     divider_mask = np.zeros((H, W), dtype=bool)\n  59:     for c in range(0, max(0, int(fc0))):\n  60:         colv = g[:, int(c)]\n  61:         non = colv[colv != int(bg)]\n  62:         if int(len(non)) >= int(H - 1) and len(set(map(int, non))) == 1:\n  63:             divider_mask[:, int(c)] = True\n  64: \n  65:     left_cols = (np.arange(W)[None, :] < int(fc0))\n  66: \n  67:     # --- sprites: per-color 8-connected components in left area (excluding divider) ---\n  68:     sprites = []\n  69:     for col in sorted(set(map(int, g.flatten())) - {int(bg)}):\n  70:         mask = (g == int(col)) & left_cols & (~divider_mask)\n  71:         if not bool(mask.any()):\n  72:             continue\n  73:         slab, sn = label_mask(mask, connectivity=8)\n  74:         for sid in range(1, int(sn) + 1):\n  75:             pts = np.argwhere(slab == int(sid))\n  76:             r0, c0 = pts.min(axis=0)\n  77:             r1, c1 = pts.max(axis=0)\n  78:             r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\n  79:             m = slab[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] == int(sid)\n  80:             sprites.append({\"color\": int(col), \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"r1\": int(r1), \"c1\": int(c1), \"mask\": m})\n  81: \n  82:     move_colors = {int(s[\"color\"]) for s in sprites}\n  83: \n  84:     # --- marker-derived targets (restricted to colors that actually appear as sprites) ---\n  85:     row_marks: dict[int, list[int]] = defaultdict(list)\n  86:     col_marks: dict[int, list[int]] = defaultdict(list)\n  87:     for r in range(int(ir0), int(ir1) + 1):\n  88:         cl = int(g[int(r), int(fc0)])\n  89:         cr = int(g[int(r), int(fc1)])\n  90:         if cl == cr and cl != int(bg) and cl in move_colors:\n  91:             row_marks[int(cl)].append(int(r))\n  92:     for c in range(int(ic0), int(ic1) + 1):\n  93:         ct = int(g[int(fr0), int(c)])\n  94:         cb = int(g[int(fr1), int(c)])\n  95:         if ct == cb and ct != int(bg) and ct in move_colors:\n  96:             col_marks[int(ct)].append(int(c))\n  97: \n  98:     targets: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n  99:     for col in set(row_marks.keys()) & set(col_marks.keys()):\n 100:         pts = {(int(r), int(c)) for r in row_marks[int(col)] for c in col_marks[int(col)]}\n 101:         targets[int(col)] = sorted(pts)\n 102: \n 103:     out = g.copy()\n 104:     movers = [s for s in sprites if int(s[\"color\"]) in targets]\n 105: \n 106:     # Erase originals of moved sprites.\n 107:     for s in movers:\n 108:         r0, c0 = int(s[\"r0\"]), int(s[\"c0\"])\n 109:         for rr, cc in np.argwhere(s[\"mask\"]):\n 110:             out[int(r0) + int(rr), int(c0) + int(cc)] = int(bg)\n 111: \n 112:     # Bottom-to-top ordering: lower sprites first, upper sprites last (covering).\n 113:     movers.sort(key=lambda s: (-((int(s[\"r0\"]) + int(s[\"r1\"])) / 2.0), int(s[\"c0\"])))\n 114: \n 115:     for s in movers:\n 116:         col = int(s[\"color\"])\n 117:         cr = (int(s[\"r0\"]) + int(s[\"r1\"])) // 2\n 118:         cc = (int(s[\"c0\"]) + int(s[\"c1\"])) // 2\n 119:         pix = [(int(s[\"r0\"]) + int(rr), int(s[\"c0\"]) + int(cc2)) for rr, cc2 in np.argwhere(s[\"mask\"])]\n 120: \n 121:         for tr, tc in targets[int(col)]:\n 122:             dr = int(tr - cr)\n 123:             dc = int(tc - cc)\n 124:             for r, c in pix:\n 125:                 rr = int(r + dr)\n 126:                 cc3 = int(c + dc)\n 127:                 if int(ir0) <= rr <= int(ir1) and int(ic0) <= cc3 <= int(ic1):\n 128:                     out[rr, cc3] = int(col)\n 129: \n 130:     return out\nEND_FUNCTION\nFUNCTION: generate_input lines 306-311\n 306: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n 307:     for _ in range(300):\n 308:         result = _gen_attempt()\n 309:         if result is not None:\n 310:             return result\n 311:     raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: failed after 300 attempts\")\nEND_FUNCTION\nEND_FILE\nFILE: /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nFUNCTION: main lines 31-83\n  31: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n  32:     \"\"\"\n  33:     Sort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n  34: \n  35:     Input structure:\n  36:     - bg = most common color.\n  37:     - Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n  38: \n  39:     Rule:\n  40:     - Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n  41:     - Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n  42:     - Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n  43:     - Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n  44: \n  45:     Key insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n  46:     \"\"\"\n  47:     g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n  48:     H, W = g.shape\n  49:     bg = int(get_bg(g))\n  50: \n  51:     comps = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=8, monochromatic=False, return_format=\"dict\")\n  52:     assert not (not comps)\n  53: \n  54:     extracted = []\n  55:     for comp in comps:\n  56:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, comp[\"bbox\"])\n  57:         patch = crop_bbox(g, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\n  58: \n  59:         # Isolate this component exactly (bbox rectangle may include background).\n  60:         rel_coords = [(int(r) - r0, int(c) - c0) for r, c in comp[\"coords\"]]\n  61:         m = mask_from_coords(rel_coords, patch.shape)\n  62:         pat = np.full_like(patch, bg)\n  63:         pat[m] = patch[m]\n  64: \n  65:         extracted.append(\n  66:             {\n  67:                 \"c0\": int(c0),\n  68:                 \"pat\": pat,\n  69:                 \"shape_key\": canonical_shape_key(m),\n  70:             }\n  71:         )\n  72: \n  73:     freq = Counter(d[\"shape_key\"] for d in extracted)\n  74:     out = np.full((H, W), int(bg), dtype=int)\n  75: \n  76:     for d in extracted:\n  77:         pat = d[\"pat\"]\n  78:         h, w = map(int, pat.shape)\n  79:         dest_r0 = 0 if int(freq[d[\"shape_key\"]]) >= 2 else int(H - h)\n  80:         dest_c0 = int(d[\"c0\"])\n  81:         paste(out, pat, dest_r0, dest_c0, transparent=int(bg))\n  82: \n  83:     return out\nEND_FUNCTION\nFUNCTION: generate_input lines 361-366\n 361: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n 362:     for _ in range(MAX_RETRIES):\n 363:         grid = _gen_attempt()\n 364:         if grid is not None:\n 365:             return grid\n 366:     raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid 62593bfd sample\")\nEND_FUNCTION\nEND_FILE\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
